<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Biophys Biochem Cytol</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of Biophysical and Biochemical Cytology</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0095-9901</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">13673046</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2229752</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>The Isolation of Nucleoli from Ungerminated Pea Embryos</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Johnston</surname><given-names>F. B.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Setterfield</surname><given-names>G.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Stern</surname><given-names>Herbert</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="N0x1c95e00N0x1d733e0">From Plant Research Institute, Central Experimental Farm, Canada Department of Agriculture, and Division of Applied Biology, National Research Council Ottawa, Canada</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>1</day><month>8</month><year>1959</year></pub-date><volume>6</volume><issue>1</issue><fpage>53</fpage><lpage>56</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>20</day><month>11</month><year>1958</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; Copyright, 1959, by The Rockefeller Institute</copyright-statement></permissions><abstract><p> A method is described for the isolation of nuclei and nucleoli from ungerminated pea embryos. Electron micrographs of the nucleolar preparation indicate a high degree of purity but only a partial preservation of composition, because of a loss of material from the nucleoli which occurs during the isolation procedure. </p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>